A||N||11||87GTBro||I don't know what's wrong...||||5||
Z||000000||87GTBro||02-19-2000||09:42 PM||||When I start my 86 GT in the morning, it runs fine.  I have no problems with it when I am starting it cold.<P>BUT... If I try to start the engine warm, it does not stay running. (Only some of the time, not always)  It starts and turns a few times and stalls out.  The exhaust fumes have a rich gasoline smell.  To me, it is acting like it is flooding itself.  I do not press on the gas peddle, just the clutch.<P>I am baffled.  Anybody know any probable causes?  It does eventually start after 20-30 minutes of trying.  (Probably enough time to cool down)  <P>Please help...||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||02-19-2000||09:47 PM||||If there is a gasoline smell, it is possible one or more fuel injectors are leaking. Since the fuel system stays pressurized, even when the ignition is off, if an injector is leaking it could/will flood that cylinder.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTBro||02-19-2000||10:17 PM||||But it only does it when the engine is warm.  If that was the case, wouldn't I have the same prolem in the morning when I try to start the car?<P>If so, you may be right.  The engine was in bad shape when I got the car.  Thanks...  It's worth checking out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||02-19-2000||10:26 PM||||If the injector(s) were leaking, by the time morning came, it could be possible the fuel pressure had dropped low enough to a point where the injectors could stay closed, and the fuel could evaporate off before you start the engine.<BR>My engine (until very recently) started immediatly on the morning cold start. But if the engine had thoroughly warmed up, and I shut it off for 30+ minutes, it takes several seconds before it fires up. If it sits several hours, it fires right up like the morning cold start.<BR>I've been told this is a common chevy 60 degree trait.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 02-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||02-19-2000||10:55 PM||||A guy at work has an '86 turbo Sunbird that was doing the same exact thing. We tried everything (tune-up, sensors, etc) It turns out his catalytic converter was "starting" to clog up. He found it by accident when he took it for his emission test, he read 3500 rpm when the exhaust machine only read 2000 rpm. The guy at the emission testing place told him to have it checked cause he'd seen the problem before. The converter was replaced and it fixed the starting problem and he passed emission testing.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||02-19-2000||11:01 PM||||One posssible way to check for a clogging cat is to thread in a pressure guage where the O2 sensor plugs in. If your over 3psi at 2000rpm(I think), that means your cat is plugging. This assumes you are able to get the O2 sensor out.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phaeton||02-20-2000||01:17 AM||||The V6 should have a 'clear flood' mode, the ecm will shut off the fuel if the tps registers WOT during engine cranking.  I guess this won't fix whatever is causing the problem though, my fingers work faster than my head at times.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||02-20-2000||08:59 AM||||I had always heard that if a WOT condition were present upon starting the cold start injector was shut off, but the regular injectors still pulsed out fuel. I could be wrong though. Its not something definite.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||02-20-2000||09:28 AM||||Stick a fuel pressure gauge on, turn the ignition to "run", wait until the f/p relay kicks off, then switch off the ignition. Watch the pressure, if it drops, there is a leak somewhere.<BR>The coil is a possibility too. I would check it out.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||02-20-2000||12:18 PM||||Get the thing hot. shut it off. unplug the cold start injector. then try it. if the cold injector switch is bad that will tell you quick.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||02-20-2000||01:52 PM||||How about the tach filter, Ogre? Is that possible?||||reg||1
Z||000011||87GTBro||02-20-2000||09:01 PM||||I went with the injector theory.  I talked to a mechanic and he said it is possible that there could be carbon build up in the tip of one or more of the injectors which would cause it to leak under pressure.  He recommended putting Berryman's B-12 Complete Fuel System Treatment in the gas.<P>I did that today, and so far everything is fine.  I hope it did the trick.||||reg||4
